\description{
Helper functions to
manipulate parameter tables. They are
exported for advanced users.
}
\details{
The function \code{\link[=combine_partables]{combine_partables()}}
combine a list of \code{partables} objects
or \code{eq_partables} objects
to one single object of the same type.

The \code{print}-method of \code{eq_partables}
object handles a zero-length list.
If not of zero-length, the \code{print}-method
for \code{partables} will be used.

The \code{c}-method of \code{eq_partables}
object combine \code{eq_partables} elements
to one single \code{eq_partables} elements.

The \code{duplicated}-method of \code{eq_partables}
checks whether any models are
identical. If yes, the duplicated
models, except for the first one,
will be denoted as duplicated.

The \code{unique}-method of \code{eq_partables}
returns an \code{eq_partables} object with
duplicated models, if any, removed.

The function \code{\link[=setdiff_eq_partables]{setdiff_eq_partables()}}
removes from \code{x} models that are
also in \code{y}.

The function \code{\link[=union_eq_partables]{union_eq_partables()}}
combines the models in \code{x} and \code{y},
with duplicated models removed.

The function \code{\link[=intersect_eq_partables]{intersect_eq_partables()}}
find the models common in \code{x} and \code{y}.

The function \code{\link[=setequal_eq_partables]{setequal_eq_partables()}}
checks whether \code{x} and \code{y} has the
same set of models. Orders are ignored.

The function \code{\link[=is_element_eq_partables]{is_element_eq_partables()}}
checks whether \code{el} is one of the
models in \code{set}.

The function \code{\link[=match_eq_partables]{match_eq_partables()}}
is similar to \code{\link[=match]{match()}}, but check
matches based on the results of
\code{\link[=get_digest]{get_digest()}} applied to the
parameter tables.

\verb{\%pt_in\%} is similar to \code{\%in\%},
but works on parameter tables using
\code{\link[=match_eq_partables]{match_eq_partables()}}.

\verb{\%pt_notin\%} is similar to \verb{\%notin\%},
but works on parameter tables using
\code{\link[=match_eq_partables]{match_eq_partables()}}.

The function \code{\link[=eq_partables]{eq_partables()}}
creates an \code{eq_partables} object
from \code{lavaan} parameter tables
or \code{lavaan} outputs.

The function \code{\link[=as_eq_partables]{as_eq_partables()}} is
not a usual \code{as} function. It works
only on a \code{lavaan} output or
\code{lavaan} parameter table. It converts
\code{sem_out} to a one-element
list of the class \code{eq_partables},
with the parameter table as the
element and the \code{lavaan} output,
if \code{sem_out} is a \code{lavaan} output, in
the attribute \code{"fit"}. If \code{sem_out}
is not a \code{lavaan} object nor a
\code{lavaan} parameter table, a zero-length
\code{eq_partables} object will be returned.

The function \code{\link[=is_partable]{is_partable()}} check
whether an object is probably a
parameter table. It checks whether
(a) the object is a \code{data.frame}-like
object (by \code{\link[=is.data.frame]{is.data.frame()}}) and (b)
the columns in \code{colchk} exist. If
both conditions are met, then the
object is considered a parameter
table.

The function \code{\link[=is_partables]{is_partables()}} checks
whether a list is likely a list of parameter
tables. It simply calls \code{\link[=is_partable]{is_partable()}}
on all the elements.
}
\examples{

library(lavaan)

# Model 1

mod1 <-
"
fx =~ x1 + x2 + x3
fm =~ m1 + m2 + m3
fy =~ y1 + y2 + y3
fm ~ fx
fy ~ fm + fx
"
fit1 <- sem(
          model = mod1,
          data = data_test_3_factor_3_item
        )
fit1_1_more <- drop_k(fit1)
fit1_1_more_1_less <- lapply(
  fit1_1_more,
  add_k
)

partables1 <- combine_partables(fit1_1_more_1_less)
partables1

}
